<BaseLayout title="Video Compression & Open Standards - François Pitié" currentSection="research">
  <div class="post">
    <header class="post-header mb-8">
      <div class="mb-2">
        <a href="/research" class="text-xs font-mono text-[var(--theme-link)] hover:text-[var(--theme-accent)]">&larr; back to research</a>
      </div>
      <h1 class="post-title">Video Compression &amp; Open Standards</h1>
      <p class="desc text-xs sm:text-sm text-[var(--theme-muted)] mt-1.5 pb-3 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
        Rate-distortion optimization, High Dynamic Range (HDR) encoding, and standards contributions to AV1 and AVM.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="space-y-10">
      <!-- Overview -->
      <section class="space-y-4 text-sm sm:text-base text-[var(--theme-fg)] leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-light tracking-tight text-[var(--theme-heading)] pb-1.5 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
          Research Overview
        </h2>
        <p>
          Video compression accounts for the vast majority of global internet traffic and forms the operational backbone of streaming media, cloud gaming, and digital film distribution. As media formats advance toward 4K/8K resolution, high dynamic range (HDR), wide colour gamuts, and high frame rates, conventional compression heuristics struggle to balance bit-rate budgets, perceptual visual fidelity, and encoding computational complexity.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our video compression research addresses this trilemma through rigorous rate-distortion optimization, perceptual quality modelling, and content-adaptive encoder control. We contribute directly to open, royalty-free international video standards and collaborate closely with industry pioneers including Google, YouTube, and Netflix.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Alliance for Open Media (AOMedia) -->
      <section class="space-y-4 text-sm sm:text-base text-[var(--theme-fg)] leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-light tracking-tight text-[var(--theme-heading)] pb-1.5 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
          International Standards: AV1 &amp; AVM
        </h2>
        <p>
          Our laboratory represents Trinity College Dublin as an Academic Member and Contributor within the <strong>Alliance for Open Media (AOMedia)</strong> Video Quality Working Group. Alongside engineering teams from Google, Netflix, and Meta, our research informs the development and evaluation of <strong>AV1</strong> and the next-generation <strong>AVM (AOMedia Video Model)</strong> codec.
        </p>
        <p>
          Recent milestones include an invited address at the <em>AOMedia Research Workshop Europe 2025</em> (co-located with QoMEX), titled <em>"Beyond Streaming: Applying AV1 to Cinema-Grade Virtual Production"</em>, demonstrating energy-efficient and ultra-high-fidelity AV1 encoding pipelines designed for professional virtual film production.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- HDR & Per-Clip Lagrangian Optimization -->
      <section class="space-y-4 text-sm sm:text-base text-[var(--theme-fg)] leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-light tracking-tight text-[var(--theme-heading)] pb-1.5 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
          HDR Compression &amp; Per-Clip Optimization
        </h2>
        <p>
          While High Dynamic Range (HDR) dramatically enhances perceived contrast and color saturation, traditional video codecs rely on Rate-Distortion Optimization (RDO) models formulated for standard dynamic range (SDR). Tuning the operational trade-off between bit-rate and distortion requires choosing the Lagrangian multiplier (<span class="font-mono text-sm">&lambda;</span>) adaptively for each video sequence.
        </p>
        <p>
          In a multi-year collaboration with Google and YouTube led by Dr. Daniel Ringis, we investigated direct closed-loop optimization of <span class="font-mono text-sm">&lambda;</span> and systematically evaluated perceptual quality metrics (including VMAF, HDR-VQM, and subjective observer trials) across large-scale 4K HDR corpuses. This work received the <strong>Top-10 Papers Award at Picture Coding Symposium (PCS 2021)</strong> and was published in the <em>IEEE Transactions on Broadcasting</em> and <em>IEEE ICIP</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Furthermore, our research in the <em>SMPTE Motion Imaging Journal</em> examined the perceptual and computational implications of video compression inside LED-wall virtual production volumes, establishing guidelines for lossless and near-lossless intra-studio streaming.
        </p>
      </section>

